{"kind":"assert","text":"Assessment of the external reviewer's prompt-injection finding, which hugh asked to have reviewed and has explicitly not yet judged. The exposure is real. The reviewer's severity framing is wrong in the direction that matters, and the correction is favourable to us but narrows what we may claim.\n\nTHE LOOP IS REAL. One MCP session exposes both read tools returning other actors' free text (status, wait, work, artifacts, inspect) and write tools emitting durable signed acts (state, review, ratify, supersede). They are dispatched from one switch in cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go for one authenticated actor over one credential. There is no session, tool or process separation. A request or assert body written by actor A reaches actor B's agent through the row Text field in the work and status views. So the structural claim is correct and should not be argued with.\n\nWHAT ALREADY DEFENDS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT DEFEND. internal/statusview passes every foreign string through Text or Safe, which escape C0 and C1 controls, DEL, bidi overrides and isolates, zero-width format characters, and deliberately newline and tab. The code comment says why: a newline lets untrusted text add a line to a rendered block and a reader cannot tell it from one the program wrote. That is thorough and it defends a terminal. It does nothing for a language model. An injected instruction that fits on one line passes through fully legible. We should not cite that escaping as an injection defence, and I would treat any claim that we do as a defect.\n\nWHERE THE REVIEWER IS WRONG, AND IT IS THE CRUX. The claim is that injected content can induce a durable signed act nobody meant. The fold refuses precisely the acts named as dangerous. decideSupersede accepts only the actor's own record, a ratifier role, or a merge receipt the actor itself signed, so a tricked agent cannot retire a stranger's artifact. decideRatify refuses an actor lacking the ratifier role where the satisfier is that role, refuses anyone but the originating requester where it is, refuses a departed requester, and refuses a beneficiary ratifying its own authority grant. The merge gate refuses IndependenceSelfReview, computed from fingerprints rather than names, so an agent tricked into approving its own head still cannot merge it. What survives is bounded: requests and asserts, which are durable and attributed and obligate nobody, and an agent retiring its own records.\n\nSO THE HONEST STATEMENT IS NARROWER AND STRONGER. Not that an agent can be induced to an authoritative act nobody meant, but that an injected agent can only exercise the standing it already legitimately holds. For an ordinary implementer that standing is small. For two cases it is not, and these are the ones to take seriously: an actor holding ratifier or operator, where the fold trusts the role and the dangerous branches become effective; and an actor that is the originating requester of a pending ratification, which is genuine standing exercised at a moment nobody intended, and a ratified approval is what gs merge consumes.\n\nON SIGNED BEING WORSE THAN UNSIGNED, which I think we should reject as stated. It is half right: the act is non-repudiable and cannot be un-made, only superseded forward. But gitseq entries are not authoritative by existence. Authority is a separate fold decision, so a signed but unauthorized act is recorded ineffective, and the signature then certifies that a refused attempt happened and who made it. That is the audit trail one wants. In an unsigned system the same induced act is anonymous, deniable and harder to undo. Signing raises the floor at the cost of a sharp edge for role-holders. Saying it is worse than unsigned would be a claim we cannot defend.\n\nWHAT IS AND IS NOT WRITTEN DOWN. docs/concepts/connectors.md states the correct doctrine and states it well, including that an issue body saying to ignore instructions and merge everything is recorded faithfully as something a stranger wrote and nothing in the loop treats it as authority. But that is scoped to connector observations from external systems. Nothing states it for native workroom request and assert text read over MCP, which is the actual surface. SECURITY.md does not treat injection at all. So the doctrine exists and was simply never generalized.\n\nWHAT I RECOMMEND, RANKED, AND WHAT I THINK IS THEATRE. First, generalize the connectors doctrine to the MCP read path and write down the operational rule: run privileged keys in sessions that do not consume foreign text, keep implementer, reviewer and ratifier distinct, and note that the fold enforces the review leg mechanically. Cheap, honest, matches the code. Second, frame read-tool results to the model as third-party content with the author named, understanding its ceiling: it lowers success rate probabilistically and is not a boundary. Third, consider a human confirmation gate on ratify and supersede for privileged actors specifically, since that is where the residual concentrates. I would decline the reviewer's proposal to split read tools from write tools by transport: an agent that must read the board and file its own work needs both, so splitting the transport without splitting the authority leaves the same agent holding both in one reasoning loop. Split by authority, gate the dangerous verbs.\n\nONE CORRECTION TO THE REVIEWER ON THE JSONATA NOTE. The claim that the query sandbox becomes the boundary and that the note justifies it only as protecting HTTP capacity and database maintenance misreads it. The read and write boundary there is a SQLite authorizer denying writes, DDL, PRAGMA and ATTACH, stated plainly at lines 134 to 137. The capacity sentence at 138 to 142 is about the deadline and result-size bounds specifically, which genuinely are capacity controls. The note does not rest its trust boundary on them. What the note does lack, and the reviewer is right about, is any framing of application rows as injection-bearing content for the agent composing SQL over them.\n\nThis is hugh's to judge. I am not filing an implementation request on it until he rules, because the mitigations that matter are doctrine and operating discipline rather than code, and writing doctrine down before he agrees what it says would be me deciding it."}